            <div xml:id="d610">

                <pb facs="0740" n="628" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg628"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.515/802: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-10-31T15:00:00+08:00">October 31, 1935—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 31—6:49 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your 289, October 30, 7 p.m.<anchor n="40" xml:id="d610-f1"/> It is currently
                    believed among American bankers and businessmen that the Chinese Government
                    plans to attach the Chinese dollar to the customs gold unit, at a rate of
                    Chinese dollars 2.50 for one gold unit. Mackay of National City Bank told me
                    that he believed the Chinese Government wanted to sell its silver holdings to
                    the United States for gold, with above end possibly in view.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
